From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: irisrun <irisrun@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 06:03:57 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 33/37] Run apps in Iris's UIKit and host SwiftUI with
 UIHostingController on Linux

On iOS a SwiftUI app is a UIKit app: App.main() runs UIApplicationMain with
SwiftUI's app delegate, whose scene delegate puts the scene's views in a
window under a UIHostingController. On Linux apps now run the same way, in
Iris's OpenUIKit, so SwiftUI and UIKit apps share one host: one Device
Protocol device, one script language and one screen description. The stdout
app is left for apps that ask for the stdout renderer.

UIHostingController's view, _UIHostingView, is a ViewRendererHost like the
stdout app's host: its content lays out in the view's bounds and safe area,
takes UIKit's traits, renders when the view lays out, and draws through the
render core into a layer of its own that the view's layer holds, so that
UIKit owns the window's layers and SwiftUI no longer replaces them. Touches
that UIKit hit-tests to the view go to SwiftUI's gestures; while one of its
text fields is focused the view is UIKit's first responder, so the keyboard
reaches SwiftUI, and a UIKit text input that takes the keyboard ends
SwiftUI's editing. In a script SwiftUI's clock moves on while a step settles
and while a finger is held, as before.

UIApplicationDelegateAdaptor makes the app's delegate on Linux too, through
the factory irisrun-ios-build gives such classes; SwiftUI's app delegate
passes UIKit's lifecycle callbacks on to it.
